---
/**
 * The open-source explainer.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS RATHER THAN A BARE LINK. The header's "Fork this site"
 * action points here, not at GitHub. A repository landing page answers "what
 * is this" badly for someone who has not already decided they want it. It
 * opens on a file tree. This page gives the short answer first and keeps the
 * repository link where nobody can miss it.
 *
 * WRITTEN AT ROUGHLY A 3RD-GRADE READING LEVEL, ON PURPOSE, and that is a
 * tighter constraint than the articles, which sit around 9th grade. Short
 * sentences, common words, one idea each. If you edit this, read it out loud
 * first: anything you stumble over is too long.
 *
 * NO EM DASHES ANYWHERE IN THE COPY. Bryce does not use them. Where a pause is
 * needed the house move is a full stop, or an ellipsis, which is what the
 * articles actually do.
 *
 * CloseBot is disclosed in the prose, per AGENTS.md. An open-source page is
 * exactly where an undisclosed recommendation would read as promotion.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import chatWidget from '../assets/media/screenshot-2026-10-02-at-11-41-52-am.png';
import widgetConversions from '../assets/media/screenshot-2026-10-02-at-11-39-52-am.png';
import { person } from '../data/person';
import { SOURCE_REPO_URL } from '../data/site';
---

<PageLayout
  seo={{
    title: 'Open Source: Fork This Site',
    description:
      'The code for this site is free to use. A test of building and running an SEO-friendly site with Claude Code. Fork it and make it your own.',
  }}
  heading="Open source"
  lede="The code for this site is free. Take it. Change the name. It's yours."
  width="wide"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    {/*
      The repository link, in the slot the portrait fills on /about/. Someone
      who clicked "Fork this site" already knows what they want. They should
      not have to read anything to find it.

      `.kicker` and `.panel-shadow` are the named classes from global.css, not
      their Tailwind longhand. The longhand also had to re-declare
      `tracking-[0.08em]`, which `text-kicker` already supplies, and
      `transition-colors`, which the base layer already applies to every `a`
      at 120ms linear -- Tailwind's version eases and covers properties
      DESIGN.md's motion rule does not permit. `text-ink` overrides `.kicker`'s
      muted colour, the same way ThemeToggle does.
    */}
    <a
      href={SOURCE_REPO_URL}
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class="kicker panel-shadow inline-block border border-line px-4 py-2 text-ink no-underline hover:border-accent hover:text-accent-hover"
    >
      View on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
    </a>

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">License</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">MIT. Free to use.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Built with</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">Astro and Cloudflare</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Cost to run</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">Just a domain</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <div class="prose">
        {/*
          {' '} before and after an inline link is deliberate. A newline there
          collapses to nothing and renders as "throughClaude Code". It is
          invisible in the source and obvious on the page.
        */}
        <p>
          This site is a test. I wanted to see if I could build a real website
          with AI doing most of the work. Not me typing code all day. An AI
          agent called{' '}
          <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>{' '}
          does the building. I tell it what I want.
        </p>

        <p>
          So far it works better than I thought it would. But there's a catch,
          and the catch is the whole point.
        </p>

        <h2>Why I made it free</h2>

        <p>
          Most SEO advice is just people talking. This is the real thing. The
          rules, the tests, and a live site you can go look at. You can check if
          it works instead of taking my word for it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can also just take it. Want a fast personal site that's set up
          right? This one already is. Swap my name for yours and you're done.
        </p>

        <h2>How it works, in plain words</h2>

        <p>
          Every page gets built ahead of time. It turns into a plain file that
          sits there and waits. When you visit, the file is already done. No
          server has to think about it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's why it loads fast. That's why it costs nothing to host. And
          that's why almost nothing can break.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here's the catch I mentioned. The site has rules, and the rules are
          not suggestions. A page can't go live without a title and a short
          description. Not "shouldn't." Can't. The site refuses to build. There
          are a few hundred checks like that. Broken links. Images with no
          description for screen readers. Pages that nothing links to.
        </p>

        <p>
          That matters a lot when AI writes the code. An AI will happily ship a
          page with no description, and nobody would notice for months. Here it
          fails right away and tells you why. The rules are what make the AI
          safe to trust.
        </p>

        <h2>Free chat box and visitor stats</h2>

        <p>
          {/* Disclosure in the prose, per AGENTS.md. Not inherited from the header. */}
          One more thing, and I should say up front that I co-founded the
          company behind it. So take it how you want.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href={person.worksFor.url} rel="noopener">{person.worksFor.name}</a>{' '}
          gives out a free Sales Pixel. Drop that one code into the site and you
          get two things.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first is a chat box. It answers questions for people while you
          sleep, and it can take their email for your newsletter.
        </p>

        {/*
          EAGER, AND IT IS THE FIRST IMAGE ON THE PAGE. tests/seo/images.spec.ts
          treats a document's first <img> as its likely LCP element and fails a
          lazy one. This page is not an article, so it does not get the cover
          exemption that rule carves out.

          The heuristic is wrong here -- both images sit two thirds of the way
          down -- but the honest fix is to teach the rule about above-the-fold
          position, not to quietly exempt this page. Sized to the reading
          column rather than the 1282px original, so eager costs little.
        */}
        <Image
          src={chatWidget}
          alt="The CloseBot chat widget open on this site, showing an AI assistant greeting a visitor and answering a message"
          widths={[320, 480, 608]}
          sizes="(max-width: 38rem) 100vw, 38rem"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          class="panel-shadow my-8 border border-line"
        />

        <p>
          You can change how it looks without logging in to CloseBot. The
          widget's styling lives in a file in this project, so an AI tool can
          edit it. I had Claude Code restyle mine to match the site. Square
          corners, my colors, no blue bubble.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second is numbers. You can see which pages people actually land
          on, and which ones get them to start a chat.
        </p>

        <Image
          src={widgetConversions}
          alt="A pie chart of chat conversions by page, with the home page the largest slice, followed by the writing index and the about page"
          widths={[320, 480, 608]}
          sizes="(max-width: 38rem) 100vw, 38rem"
          loading="lazy"
          class="panel-shadow my-8 border border-line"
        />

        <p>
          You don't have to use it. Leave it blank and there's just no chat box.
          Everything else works the same. But it's free, it's one line, and it
          covers two jobs most people pay for separately.
        </p>

        <h2>What's yours and what's mine</h2>

        <p>
          The code is MIT licensed. That's a fancy way of saying use it, change
          it, even sell what you make. Just keep the license file in there.
        </p>

        <p>
          My posts, my photo, and the share images with my name on them are not
          included. You'd swap those out anyway. The project lists exactly what
          to change. You get the engine, not my face.
        </p>

        <h2>How to start</h2>

        <p>
          Copy it down and run two commands. That's it. It's running on your
          computer.
        </p>

        <p>
          The setup guide has a part written just for AI agents. Point Claude
          Code at this project and the first thing it reads is the rulebook. So
          it won't have to guess.
        </p>

        <p>
          Build something with it? I'd love to see it.{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${person.email}`}>{person.email}</a>
        </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
